privacyfox (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Real correctness bug found and fixed, worked through together:
    policies.json was being installed to
    <librewolf install dir>/distribution/policies.json -- confirmed via
    dpkg -S that this path is real content the librewolf .deb itself
    ships, and confirmed via dpkg-query that it is NOT a registered
    conffile. That means it had zero protection from dpkg: the next
    ordinary "apt upgrade" pulling a newer librewolf version would
    have silently overwritten it back to LibreWolf's stock file,
    quietly undoing every bit of this project's hardening with no
    warning at all.

    Fixed by switching to /etc/librewolf/policies/policies.json --
    the other real location Firefox's policy engine reads (confirmed
    from Mozilla's own docs), genuinely unclaimed by any package
    (confirmed: /etc/librewolf didn't exist before this ran). Nothing
    else can ever silently clobber it again.

  * Added a real postrm maintainer script. Purging the package
    (apt purge privacyfox, not plain remove -- standard Debian
    convention, matches "remove keeps state for a possible reinstall,
    purge means get rid of everything") now actually deletes
    policies.json and any backups, cleanly reverting LibreWolf to its
    own stock, unhardened behavior. Previously there was no removal
    script at all -- purging left the system policy behind forever.

  * postinst simplified as a side effect -- no longer needs to detect
    where LibreWolf is installed at all, since the target path doesn't
    depend on it (Depends: librewolf already guarantees it's present).

 -- PrivacyFox Project <hello@privacyfox.dev>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:26:46 -0500

privacyfox (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Target switched from Waterfox to LibreWolf entirely. Root cause:
    Waterfox's own native ad-blocking engine has a startup-timing
    conflict with policy-driven extension installs (the
    "keep built-in blocker?" dialog from 0.1.2-0.1.4) that turned out
    to be structural, not fixable by any combination of Preferences
    policy values -- three real attempts, three failures, each with
    logic that looked correct from reading the source. LibreWolf has
    no native ad-blocker to conflict with at all (ships uBlock Origin
    itself by default), so the entire bug category doesn't exist
    there.

  * PrivacyFox.js rebuilt from scratch as a real, proper diff against
    LibreWolf's own actual hardening (librewolf.cfg 8.6, verified
    against their live upstream repo) -- not Arkenfox+Betterfox
    layered blindly on top of it. Of 207 Arkenfox+Betterfox prefs, 118
    already overlap with LibreWolf's own file (115 agree outright,
    kept as LibreWolf's own); 3 are genuine, deliberate disagreements
    (WebRTC ICE address handling, tracking-protection convenience
    allowlist, formless login capture) where LibreWolf's own choice is
    treated as intentional and NOT overridden. The remaining 89 prefs,
    found nowhere in LibreWolf's own file, are this file's actual
    content -- documented in full in the file's own header.

  * policies.json rebuilt as a real merge into LibreWolf's own current
    upstream file (not a from-scratch file the way Waterfox's was,
    since LibreWolf already ships its own) -- their uBlock Origin
    entry left exactly as they have it (normal_installed, not
    force-installed or duplicated), six more extensions added
    alongside it, DisableFirefoxAccounts added (the one thing their
    own file doesn't already set that this project cares about).

  * install.sh / privacyfox-apply: LibreWolf's real install path
    (/usr/share/librewolf, confirmed on real hardware) and profile
    resolution (legacy ~/.librewolf checked first, XDG
    ~/.config/librewolf/librewolf fallback only if that doesn't
    exist -- reusing PrivacyOS's own already-proven real-hardware
    logic for this exact question). Auto-install-if-missing rebuilt
    around LibreWolf's real, genuinely simpler install method (extrepo,
    Debian's own trusted-repo tool, three universal commands, no
    per-OS-version branching needed at all -- unlike Waterfox's).

  * userContent.css trimmed to just the one genuinely dead menu entry
    (Account & Sync). Dropped the About-page/support-link hides --
    those pointed at LibreWolf's own real project links, not vendor
    branding to distance from; this project builds on LibreWolf, it
    doesn't hide it.

  * Package now Depends: librewolf, not waterfox.

 -- PrivacyFox Project <hello@privacyfox.dev>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:09:41 -0500

privacyfox (0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Real, deterministic fix for the install-time "keep built-in
    blocker?" dialog -- supersedes 0.1.3's speculative attempt
    (dismissedExtensionInstallWarnings), which is removed as
    redundant now that the actual root cause is fixed properly.

    Traced the exact guard logic in Waterfox's own source
    (WaterfoxBlockerExtensionDetector.sys.mjs): every one of its
    detection/notification/install-warning functions checks
    waterfox.blocker.coexist FIRST, unconditionally, before touching
    anything else -- when true, they all return immediately. Setting
    it via policy (locked true) means none of that machinery ever
    runs at all, rather than racing a value it reads elsewhere. The
    native blocker itself stays genuinely, permanently off via the
    separately-locked waterfox.blocker.enabled -- this just also
    stops Waterfox's own nanny-logic from reacting to that at all.

    Real user testing (multiple rounds, working through this
    together) is what surfaced this needed a real fix rather than
    being documented away as cosmetic -- a public tool showing
    unexplained warnings on first launch, that LibreWolf/Waterfox
    themselves never show, undermines trust regardless of whether the
    underlying protection already works.

 -- PrivacyFox Project <hello@privacyfox.dev>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 11:27:22 -0500

privacyfox (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Attempt to suppress Waterfox's install-time "keep built-in
    blocker?" dialog for uBlock Origin by preemptively setting
    waterfox.blocker.dismissedExtensionInstallWarnings via policy.
    Honest caveat: this may hit the exact same startup-timing race
    that let the dialog through in the first place (both prefs apply
    via the same Preferences policy mechanism, at the same moment) --
    worth trying since it's low-cost, not guaranteed to fully resolve
    it. Confirmed real-world testing showed the underlying protection
    already works correctly even when the dialog appears (the
    built-in blocker stays locked off regardless of which button
    gets clicked) -- this only targets the cosmetic dialog itself.

 -- PrivacyFox Project <hello@privacyfox.dev>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 11:08:04 -0500

privacyfox (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Disable Waterfox's native ad-blocking engine entirely via policy
    (waterfox.blocker.enabled/ui.enabled, locked false), in favor of
    the force-installed uBlock Origin extension. Previously relied on
    Waterfox's own automatic extension-conflict detection to sort
    this out reactively after install, which left a leftover toolbar
    indicator and an unexplained prompt. Fixing it at the policy
    level, before first launch, is the correct root-cause fix.

